>
>READ THIS:
>    The Scarborough College Computer Centre facilities are solely to be
>    used for Academic purposes.

Freedom of expression, privacy, due process, participation, and moral
rights are necessary for a university to meet its academic purpose.
This policy hurts rather than helps the University's academic purpose.

>    All students with accounts on these
>    machines have been granted them solely for the purpose of furthering
>    their academic pursuits. The University owns the computer systems, the
>    accounts, the files, and the network.

By definition, if the Univeristy owns the files, then it can sell
them. Is this really that the University means to claim? Claiming
ownership of someone else's intellectual material is a violation of
the moral rights of the material's creator. It is called plagiarism.

> There is no intrinsic right to
>    privacy on these computer systems.

Is this consistent with general University privacy policy? Did the
University Senate agree with this?

> The University reserves the right to
>    audit any and all files on the system.

So any computer operator can (on his or her own) look at (for example)
confidential on-line records of experiments on human subjects. If this
is true, the Univeristy should lose its accreditation.

> Any files which are deemed
>    "unacademic" in nature may and will be immediately purged.

So the University has now given computer operators authority to decide
on their own what is "academic" and what is not. What if the professor
whose material is "immediately purged" disagrees? I guess he or she
can't appeal since the material will already be gone.

[...]
>    B.5.c. Subverting a University of Toronto computer system is a very 
>    serious offense and will be dealt with severely. If your account is 
>    revoked, you will have to find alternative ways to complete your courses.

I hope that accounts will not be revoked without due process.

>WHAT DOES THIS MEAN?
[...]
>    4) You are not allowed to talk, telnet, or ftp to computers not on
>    Scarborough Campus. You are not allowed to possess pornography in
>    any form, files which are unacademic, files which are to used for
>    subverting the system, files which may be deemed offensive by
>    any one person, or one group. Needless to say, you are not 
>	allowed to use anyone else's account (anywhere in the world)!
[...]

The policy is deemed offensive by me. I am "any one person". I expect
all the files that contain this policy to be "immediately purged" and
the policy's creator to punished.


This is perhaps the worst computer policy that I have every read (and
I've read and critiqued dozens). By its own terms, this policy
violates itself. It is vague, contradictory, and in violation of all
most all principles of academic freedom. It should be revokes.

A new policy should be created with the participation of the
university community.

- Carl

